# Project Overview
You are building "ShieldMCP" — a web-based MCP (Model Context Protocol) 
Security Scanner and Continuous Monitoring Dashboard.

## What This App Does
ShieldMCP lets developers paste their MCP server configuration (JSON) 
and instantly get a security grade (A through F) showing vulnerabilities 
in their MCP servers. It checks for:
- Hardcoded secrets/API keys in configs
- Known CVE vulnerabilities
- Missing authentication
- Tool poisoning (malicious hidden instructions)
- Path traversal risks
- Command injection risks

Paid users get continuous monitoring: daily re-scans with email alerts 
when new vulnerabilities are found.

## Tech Stack (DO NOT DEVIATE)
- Framework: Next.js 15 (App Router ONLY — no Pages Router)
- Language: TypeScript (strict mode)
- Styling: Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui components
- Database: Supabase (PostgreSQL + Auth + Row Level Security)
- Payments: Stripe (Checkout + Customer Portal + Webhooks)
- Email: Resend (for alerts)
- Hosting: Vercel
- State Management: React Server Components where possible, 
  client components only when interactivity is required

## Architecture Rules
- Use the app/ directory for all routes
- Server Components by default; add "use client" ONLY when needed
- All database calls go through Supabase client
- API routes in app/api/ directory
- Use Zod for all input validation
- Use TypeScript interfaces for all data types
- Environment variables for ALL secrets (never hardcode)
- Use loading.tsx and error.tsx for each route segment

## Database Conventions
- Table names: snake_case, plural (e.g., scans, alerts, profiles)
- Column names: snake_case
- Always include: id (UUID), created_at (timestamptz)
- Enable Row Level Security on ALL tables
- Foreign keys reference profiles(id) for user ownership

## Component Conventions
- One component per file
- File names: kebab-case (e.g., scan-results-card.tsx)
- Component names: PascalCase (e.g., ScanResultsCard)
- Use shadcn/ui for all UI elements
- Props defined with TypeScript interface above component

## Code Style
- Prefer const over let
- Arrow functions for components and handlers
- Async/await over .then() chains
- Early returns for guard clauses
- Descriptive variable names (no single letters except in loops)
- Comments only for "why", not "what"

## Error Handling
- Try/catch in all async functions
- User-friendly error messages (never expose stack traces)
- Toast notifications for user actions via sonner
- Console.error for debugging only in development

## Security
- Validate ALL user input with Zod before processing
- Sanitize any output displayed from user-submitted data
- Use Supabase RLS policies — never trust client-side auth alone
- Rate limit API routes
- NEVER store API keys or secrets in code

## File Structure
app/
├── (marketing)/          # Landing page, pricing
│   ├── page.tsx           # Homepage
│   └── pricing/page.tsx   # Pricing page
├── (auth)/               # Auth routes
│   ├── login/page.tsx
│   └── signup/page.tsx
├── (dashboard)/          # Protected app routes
│   ├── layout.tsx         # Dashboard layout with sidebar
│   ├── dashboard/page.tsx # Main dashboard
│   ├── scan/page.tsx      # New scan page
│   ├── reports/page.tsx   # Scan history
│   ├── monitors/page.tsx  # Continuous monitoring
│   ├── alerts/page.tsx    # Alert center
│   └── settings/page.tsx  # Account settings
├── api/
│   ├── scan/route.ts      # POST: Run security scan
│   ├── webhooks/
│   │   └── stripe/route.ts # Stripe webhooks
│   └── cron/
│       └── monitor/route.ts # Daily monitoring cron
├── layout.tsx             # Root layout
└── globals.css            # Global styles

## Important Context
- This is a freemium SaaS: free users get 3 scans/month, 
  Pro users ($29/mo) get unlimited scans + continuous monitoring
- The primary user is a developer who uses AI coding tools 
  (Cursor, Claude, GitHub Copilot) with MCP servers
- The security scan parses JSON config files and checks each 
  MCP server for known vulnerability patterns
- Keep the UI clean, professional, and developer-focused
- Mobile responsiveness is secondary — desktop-first